concern: `if dep not in graph` requires hashable deps; they are since dict keys. Good.\n\nDo we check missing deps before or as we go? Before any output computation — yes, first pass.\n\nPotential test expectation: KeyError args containing the missing node. `raise KeyError(dep)` gives `args=(dep,)`. Good. Could also use `raise KeyError(f\"...\")` but passing the node itself is more idiomatic.\n\nValueError message: something descriptive. Tests likely use `pytest.raises(ValueError)`.\n\nNon-mutation: we never touch graph; we build new structures. The input graph's lists aren't sorted/modified. Good.\n\nNow let me also consider: should lexicographic comparison use string sort or natural ordering? For strings, heap order is lexicographic by Unicode code points. Good.\n\nComplexity: O(V + E) plus O(V log V) for heap operations. \n\nLet me write the file, then test thoroughly with additional checks:\n- public test\n- lexicographic choice: {'a': [], 'b': [], 'c': ['a','b']} → ['a','b','c']; {'c':['a'],'b':[],'a':[]} should give ['a','b','c'] (insertion order c,b,a — old code would give b,a,c? Let's see old: first pass a ready? iterating c: deps a not done → skip; b: ready → out b; a: ready → out a; next pass c ready → out c → ['b','a','c']; correct lexicographic: ready nodes initially {a,b}, min 'a', then 'b' ready too so min('b','c'? c not ready) → b, then c → ['a','b','c'].)\n- cycle → ValueError\n- missing dep → KeyError\n- empty graph → []\n- non-mutation: deepcopy comparison\n- larger random graphs vs brute-force reference implementation\n- duplicate deps\n\nLet me write the implementation now.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"No taste file exists, so I'll"}]}}
