{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Line 8 is: `assert schedule({'z': [], 'a': ['y'], 'y': [], 'b': []}) == ['b', 'y', 'z', 'a']`.\n\nLet me recompute. Graph: z:[], a:['y'], y:[], b:[]. Ready initially: z, y, b (indegree 0). Lexicographic smallest: 'b' (b < y < z). Pop b. Ready = {y, z} → min 'y'. Pop y. Then a's indegree drops to 0 → ready {a, z} → min 'a'! a < z. So order is ['b', 'y', 'a', 'z']. My expected value was wrong, not the implementation. 'a' becomes ready before z is popped, and 'a' < 'z'.\n\nLet me fix the expectation to ['b', 'y', 'a', 'z']. Then rerun.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"That assertion was my mistake — after `y` is processed, `a` becomes ready and `'a' < 'z'`, so the correct order is `['b','y','a','z']`. Let me correct the expectation and rerun."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"[CALL_00066]","name":"shell_command","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport copy, random\nfrom scheduler import schedule\n\nassert schedule({'b': ['a'], 'a': []}) == ['a', 'b']\nassert schedule({'c': ['a'], 'b': [], 'a': []}) == ['a', 'b', 'c']\nassert schedule({'c': ['a', 'b'], 'b': ['a'], 'a': []}) == ['a', 'b', 'c']\n# ties: only-ready nodes considered each step; 'a' becomes ready after 'y' and beats 'z'\nassert schedule({'z': [], 'a': ['y'], 'y': [], 'b': []}) == ['b', 'y', 'a', 'z']\nassert schedule({}) == []\nassert schedule({'d': [], 'c': [], 'b': [], 'a': []}) == ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd']\n# duplicate/set deps handled idempotently\nassert schedule({'a': ['b', 'b'], 'b': []}) == ['b', 'a']\nassert schedule({'a': {'b'}, 'b': []}) == ['b', 'a']\n# cycle -> ValueError\nfor cyc in [{'a': ['b'], 'b': ['a']}, {'a': ['a']}, {'a': ['b'], 'b': ['c'], 'c': ['a'], 'd': []}]:\n    try:\n        schedule(cyc)\n        raise AssertionError('expected ValueError for %r' % (cyc,))\n    except ValueError:\n        pass\n# missing dep -> KeyError carrying the missing node\nfor bad, missing in [({'a': ['x']}, 'x'), ({'a': [], 'b': ['a', 'missing']}, 'missing')]:\n    try:\n        schedule(bad)\n        raise AssertionError('expected KeyError for %r' % (bad,))\n    except KeyError as e:\n        assert e.args[0] == missing, e\n# KeyError takes precedence over cycle when both present\ntry:\n    schedule({'a': ['b'], 'b': ['a'], 'c': ['nope']})\n    raise AssertionError('expected KeyError')\nexcept KeyError:\n    pass\n# non-mutation\ng = {'c': ['a', 'b'], 'b': ['a'], 'a': []}\nsnap = copy.deepcopy(g)\nschedule(g)\nassert g == snap, g\n\n# randomized DAGs vs brute-force lexicographic reference\ndef brute(graph):\n    done, out = set(), []\n    while len(done) < len(graph):\n        ready = sorted(n for n, d in graph.items() if n not in done and all(x in done for x in d))\n        assert ready\n        done.add(ready[0]); out.append(ready[0])\n    return out\n\nrandom.seed(0)\nfor _ in range(1000):\n    n = random.randint(0, 30)\n    names = ['n%02d' % i for i in range(n)]\n    g = {name: random.sample(names[:i], random.randint(0, i)) for i, name in enumerate(names)}\n    assert schedule(g) == brute(g), g\n# random possibly-cyclic graphs: valid lexicographic topo order or ValueError, never hang\nfor _ in range(1000):\n    n = random.randint(1, 12)\n    names = ['n%02d' % i for i in range(n)]\n    g = {x: random.sample(names, random.randint(0, min(3, n))) for x in names}\n    try:\n        res = schedule(g)\n        assert sorted(res) == sorted(names)\n        assert res == brute(g)\n    except ValueError:\n        # confirm a cycle genuinely exists\n        remaining = dict(g)\n        changed = True\n        while changed:\n            changed = False\n            for x in list(remaining):\n                if all(d not in remaining for d in remaining[x]):\n                    del remaining[x]; changed = True\n        assert remaining, g\nprint('ALL_CHECKS_OK')\nEOF","description":"Run corrected comprehensive scheduler verification"}}]}}
